      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "An 83-year-old man with left renal cell carcinoma (RCC; pT4N0M0) was treated with postoperative combined subcutaneous injection therapy of alpha interferon (IFN-alpha) and IFN gamma-1a (IFN-gamma-1a). Metastasis to the pleura occurred 3 months after surgery. The metastatic lesion grew while the treatment was changed to intramuscular injection of IFN-alpha-2b due to the presence of severe general malaise, which seemed to be caused by IFN-alpha and IFN-gamma therapy. As melosalgia associated with sciatica was also severe, treatment with meloxicam, which is known as a potent cyclooxigenase-2 inhibitor among commercially available non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, was combined, resulting in significant improvement in activity of daily life, 43.2% decrease in the size of the pleural metastasis and complete regression of retroperitoneal residual tumor."},
